(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-03"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Atoms join to form molecules and crystals because bonding lowers their energy. This chapter covers ionic and covalent bonds and the octet rule (Kossel and Lewis), shapes of molecules from VSEPR theory, valence bond theory with hybridisation, molecular orbital theory, bond parameters, dipole moment and resonance."},"5.2":{"s":"Kossel and Lewis explained bonding by the stable octet of a noble gas. An ionic bond forms by the transfer of electrons to give cations and anions held by electrostatic attraction; its strength is measured by lattice enthalpy, higher for small, highly charged ions. A covalent bond is a shared electron pair. Lewis dot structures show valence electrons, and formal charge helps pick the best structure. The octet rule has limits.","f":[{"x":"Formal charge = V - N - B/2","n":"V = valence electrons of the free atom, N = non-bonding (lone-pair) electrons on it, B = bonding electrons in the structure.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 5.2, p.59"}],"w":["The best Lewis structure has formal charges as close to zero as possible, with any negative charge on the more electronegative atom.","Lattice enthalpy rises with higher ion charge and smaller ionic size: AlCl₃ > CaCl₂ > NaCl."]},"5.3":{"s":"VSEPR theory says the shape of a molecule depends on the repulsion between the electron pairs around the central atom: they arrange themselves as far apart as possible. Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs, which squeezes the bond angles. With no lone pairs the shapes are linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal and octahedral. Lone pairs change the shape: NH₃ is pyramidal and H₂O is bent.","w":["Order of repulsion: lone pair-lone pair > lone pair-bond pair > bond pair-bond pair.","NH₃ (107°) and H₂O (104.5°) have bond angles below the tetrahedral 109.5° because of their lone pairs."]},"5.4":{"s":"Valence bond theory says a covalent bond forms by the overlap of half-filled atomic orbitals, and the greater the overlap the stronger the bond. Head-on overlap along the bond axis gives a σ bond; sideways overlap of p orbitals gives a π bond. Hybridisation mixes atomic orbitals into equivalent hybrid orbitals (sp, sp², sp³ and others) that explain the shapes of molecules such as methane, ethene and ethyne.","w":["A σ bond is formed first; a double bond is one σ and one π bond, and a triple bond is one σ and two π bonds.","sp = linear (180°), sp² = trigonal planar (120°), sp³ = tetrahedral (109.5°)."]},"5.5":{"s":"Molecular orbital theory says that atomic orbitals combine to give molecular orbitals that belong to the whole molecule. Two atomic orbitals give a lower-energy bonding orbital and a higher-energy antibonding orbital. Electrons fill the molecular orbitals by the same rules as in atoms. The bond order is half the difference between the numbers of bonding and antibonding electrons. This explains why O₂ is paramagnetic.","f":[{"x":"Bond order = (N_b - N_a)/2","n":"N_b = electrons in bonding molecular orbitals, N_a = electrons in antibonding molecular orbitals. A positive bond order means a stable molecule.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 5.5, p.72"}],"w":["A bond order of zero means no stable molecule: He₂ does not exist.","More unpaired electrons in molecular orbitals means paramagnetic; O₂ has two."]},"5.6":{"s":"Covalent bonds are described by their bond angle, bond enthalpy, bond length, bond order and polarity. Bond length is the equilibrium distance between the two nuclei; it falls as bond order rises. Bond enthalpy is the energy needed to break one mole of bonds in gaseous molecules; stronger bonds have larger values. Bond order is the number of bonds between two atoms. Polarity comes from different electronegativities.","w":["Higher bond order means a shorter bond and a higher bond enthalpy: C≡C is shorter and stronger than C=C, which is shorter and stronger than C–C."]},"5.7":{"s":"In a polar bond the more electronegative atom has a partial negative charge, so the bond has a dipole moment. The dipole moment is the product of the charge and the distance between the charges, and it is a vector pointing towards the negative end. A molecule's dipole moment is the vector sum of its bond dipoles and lone-pair contributions. Symmetric molecules like BeF₂ and BF₃ have zero net dipole moment although each bond is polar.","f":[{"x":"mu = Q*r","n":"Dipole moment: Q = magnitude of charge, r = distance between the charges; unit debye (D), 1 D = 3.336 × 10^-30 C m.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 5.7, p.76"}],"w":["A molecule with polar bonds can still be non-polar if the dipoles cancel by symmetry (CO₂, BF₃, CCl₄)."]},"5.8":{"s":"When one Lewis structure cannot describe a molecule, several structures, the canonical forms, are drawn that differ only in the positions of electrons. The real molecule is their resonance hybrid, which is more stable than any one of them. The difference in energy is the resonance energy. Ozone and carbonate show this, with all the bond lengths equal. Ionic bonds can also have some covalent character, as Fajans' rules explain.","w":["Resonance forms are not different molecules that interconvert; the real molecule is a single hybrid structure.","A small, highly charged cation and a large anion make a bond more covalent."]}};
